BUILDING
========

Odin is built on GNU/Linux with GCC (C++20), GNU make and pkg-config.
It needs the development files of SDL 3, GLEW and Jansson, and a graphics
driver that offers OpenGL 4.3.

 $ cd path/to/odin
 $ make

This makes bin/odin. An object is built again when a header it includes
changes. "make install" copies the binary to ~/.local/bin, and to
~/.local/share/odin the list of the games, odin-games.json, with the lists of
the mesh programs of each game (shaders/).


RUNNING
=======

 $ bin/odin -R <ROM directory> -r <game>

(-R may be left out once the menu, page Paths, keeps the folder: roms in
[paths] of odin.ini.) Without -r, as a double click starts it, Odin opens
its library: the games of the list, those whose files are missing said so
with the files and the sets of MAME that hold them, and buttons to choose
the folders of the games and of the BIOS (kept in [paths]); the arrows or a
click choose a game, Enter or a second click plays it, and when it ends (but
by Ctrl+Q, which quits) the library comes back. The pictures of the games
are in the folder of the artwork (artwork/ in the data folder of Odin,
beside saves/; artwork in [paths], page Paths; ODIN_ARTWORK=<folder> another
one, none none), named after the game in MAME, in any case:
<game>_title.png, the title Odin keeps the first time the game shows it with
no key or button pressed since its power-on, is its picture in the library;
<game>_logo.png, a picture of the user, is shown there instead;
<game>_bezel.png is laid over the window where it is wider than the screen
of the game, the game in its hole: the part not opaque joined to its middle
(the glass of a screen often is half so), or as the <game>_bezel.info of
Batocera beside it says (swracer_logo.png, swracer_bezel.png). bezel in
[display] (page Display) says what is around the game there: off, black;
blur, the game blurred and dimmer; image, its bezel, black without one. The
games are those of odin-games.json, by the names of their MAME sets:
airtrix, braveff, pharrier, sgnascar, swracer and von4. The file is looked
for in the current directory, then in $HOME, then in ~/.local/share/odin.
The ROM directory holds each game as MAME keeps it: a directory, a zip or a
7z of the name of the game, with the files odin-games.json lists for it. The
files a game shares with others, in the sets of MAME hikaru (the BIOS), mie,
jvs13551 and 18wheelr, are looked for in the BIOS directory (-B <path>, bios
in [paths], page Paths), then in the ROM directory: hikaru.zip, hikaru.7z or
a directory hikaru; they may be in the directory or the archive of the game
too. A file is the one of its size and of the CRC MAME gives it: in an
archive by its CRC, whatever its name; in a directory by its name, with or
without dashes, in any case, else by its CRC. One found by its name with
another CRC is said, and taken (another dump of the chip). When a file is
missing, Odin says which, and the set of MAME that holds it. The directories
may have names in any language (on Windows, 10 version 1903 or later); the
menu writes them with the fonts the system has: on Windows those it comes
with, on Linux DejaVu Sans and, for Chinese, Japanese and Korean, Noto Sans
CJK, Droid Sans Fallback or WenQuanYi. The programs of the I/O boards of
SWRACER (315-6215.bin) and NASCAR (epr-21868.ic7, from the 18wheelr set of
MAME) may be left out: an imitation of the board answers then, made from
that program and checked against it. The games play the same without them,
and NASCAR sends its wheel (the force feedback) the same orders; with them
the board runs its own program, a little slower.

Options:

 -n <frame>		stops after that frame
 -s <frame>[,...]	saves <game>-<frame>.odinstate after these frames
 -l <file>		loads a state at startup
 -u			lifts the limit of 60 frames per second
 -x <num>		draws the 3D num times finer than 640 by 480 (1 to 6)
 -a <num>		filters the textures with an anisotropy of num (2 to
			16); the board has none
 -w <num>		makes the window num times 640 by 480
 -f			takes the whole display (F11 or Alt+Enter goes back
			and forth)
 -v			says each access of the board (slow; also "verbose = on"
			in [log] of odin.ini, page Paths of the menu)

Keys: Escape or F10 opens the menu, which pauses the game (its Display
page sets the window and the picture, kept in [display] of odin.ini:
scale = 1 to 6, anisotropy = 1 to 16, window = 1 to 6, vsync = off, on or
adaptive (the frames on time wait for the display, a late one does not),
fullscreen, unthrottled and show_speed = on or off; the options say the
same and come first; no vsync at an unlimited pace, nor vsync = on on a
display slower than 60 frames a second),
Ctrl+Q quits, P pauses, F4 saves <game>.odinstate and F1 loads it, in
~/.local/share/odin/states (states in [paths] of odin.ini, page Paths),
F11 or Alt+Enter takes the whole display. The page Hotkeys of the menu
changes these keys and the buttons below, kept in [hotkeys] of odin.ini:

 [hotkeys]
 pause = Pause, pad:guide+y

(menu, pause, save_state, load_state, fullscreen and quit: two keys at
most, Ctrl+Q written so, and a button held with Guide, pad:guide+<button>,
or pad:guide for Guide alone; none for nothing.)

0 is TEST and 9 SERVICE, the buttons of the cabinet: they open and work
the menu of the BOOTROM and the test menus of the games (both together
leave INPUT TEST); 5 and 6 are the coins. Player 1
has 1 for Start, the arrows, and Left Ctrl, Left Alt, Left Shift, Z, X, C;
player 2 has 2 for Start, R F D G, and A, S, Q, W, E, T. The arrows also
move a wheel, a stick or a sight, a step each frame, and back when let go;
Up is the gas and Down the brake; the second stick of player 1 (ROLL of
Air Trix, the right lever of Racer) is on R F D G. X and C shift the gears
down and up. Virtual-On has the keys of MAME: E S D F the left lever, I J
K L the right one, Left Ctrl and Left Alt the shot and the dash of the
left lever, Space and Left Shift those of the right one.

In Brave the pad of a player shakes while the nozzle of the cabinet does
(Vibration in the advanced options of the page Input, or "vibration =
off" in [players.braveff], stops it).
The lamps and the other outputs of the cabinets go to other programs (a
lamp or a force feedback program) as MAME gives them with its network
output, when odin.ini says so (the menu, page Outputs, turns it on and
off, and shows the outputs of each game light):

 [outputs]
 network = on

(port 8000 of this computer only: "mame_start = <game>", then "<id> = 1"
or "<id> = 0" as each output of the game changes, its id in
odin-games.json, and "mame_stop = 1" at the end.)

NASCAR and Planet Harriers have a second sound board for speakers of
their own (the backrest of the seat). Odin plays the two boards on two
speakers, the second one at the level odin-games.json gives it
(option_level, in dB), as the volumes of the cabinet set it; odin.ini
turns that knob, in the section of the game:

 [sound.sgnascar]
 option_level = -15

(-60 to 12 dB; -o quad plays the four channels apart, -o main and
-o option one board alone; "mode = quad" or "mode = main" in that section
too.) The menu, page Audio, sets them, and in [sound] the device the sound
plays on, the volume of Odin and its sound while the window is in the
background:

 [sound]
 device = <a name the page lists>
 volume = 80
 background = off
 In Racer the second board feeds the vibration
device of the control panel, not speakers: the pad of player 1 shakes as
hard as its sound; the page Audio plays it in the speakers too, where the
user wants it ("mode = mix", -13 dB by default, or "mode = quad" in
[sound.swracer]). In NASCAR the pad of player 1 shakes with the shocks
the game gives the wheel, not with the hold and the damping that follow
the moves of the wheel (experimental; Vibration in the advanced options
of the page Input, or "vibration = off" in [players.swracer] or
[players.sgnascar], stops it).

The mouse aims for player 1 in Brave: the sight where it points on the
picture, water on the left button, fog on the right one, Start on the
middle one; off the picture, the nozzle points away from the screen. Of
the keys, a stick and the mouse, the last that moved works a control. The
sight meets the pointer once NOZZLE SENSOR SETTING (in the test menu of
the game) has been done with the mouse.

Gamepads: one for each player, in the order they are plugged in, or the
one chosen for the game in the menu (Input). SELECT (SHARE) puts in a
coin for the player of the pad. Guide alone opens the menu; Guide held
with Back pauses, with RB saves the state, with LB loads it, with Start
quits (a button pressed with Guide is not the game's), with the left stick
pressed is SERVICE, with the right one TEST. Start, the D-pad, A, B,
X, Y, LB, RB, LT, RT, the sticks and the triggers work the controls of
each game as their roles tell (odin-games.json): for NASCAR,
the wheel on the left stick, the pedals on the triggers, the gears on LB
and RB, the view change on Y (or Z); for Brave, the sight where the left
stick points, the water on RT and the fog on LT; for Planet Harriers,
BULLET on RT, MISSILE on LT and BOMB on A; for Racer, BRAKE on RT and
BOOST on A. Every control of a game can take other keys and pad inputs
in odin.ini, beside the saves (see below), in the section of that game,
under the id odin-games.json gives it:

 [controls.sgnascar]
 view_change = key:Space, pad:b
 gear_up = pad:a
 coin1 = key:5

 [controls.pharrier]
 stick_v = pad:lefty:invert
 stick_h_left = key:A
 stick_h_right = key:D

(pad:-lefty is the left stick up, pad:+leftx the stick to the right,
mouse:left, mouse:middle, mouse:right its buttons, mouse:x and mouse:y
where it points, mouse:off away from the picture;
pad:lefty:invert the whole axis turned over: Planet Harriers then goes up
when the stick goes up, where its cabinet stick, a flight stick, goes up
when pulled.) An analog control takes the axes of a pad on its line, and
keys and buttons on the halves of its way: <id>_left and <id>_right, or
<id>_up and <id>_down; a pedal takes them on its own line, with its axes
(gas_pedal = pad:righttrigger, key:Up).

(pad:guide+leftstick is a button held with Guide.) The menu (Escape, F10
or Guide) writes these lines itself, and the gamepad chosen for a player:

 [players.braveff]
 p1 = 030000005e0400008e02000014010000 Xbox 360 Controller

and for the gamepads the dead zone of their sticks, in % of the way (a
circle round the centre where a stick gives nothing; past it the way
starts from 0), and how far a trigger goes before it presses a button
(the advanced options of the page Input; 0 to 30 and 0 to 50):

 [gamepads]
 dead_zone = 3
 trigger_threshold = 3

a make and model its own in [gamepad.<GUID>], the GUID of its line in
[players.<game>]:

 [gamepad.030000005e0400008e02000014010000]
 dead_zone = 8

and the controls of the page Input as a list rather than on the drawing
(the button Classic):

 [menu]
 classic = on

(animations = off: in a small window the row of the games, a name
between dots, changes at once rather than moving.)

In the menu, LB and RB of a gamepad change the page, LT and RT the game
(on the page Input they are tested as the controls they work: there the
first two rows of the frame, the tabs and the games, change them), B goes
back (a list dropped open closes first, then the advanced options, a
key caught is let go, else the game goes on). The D-pad or the left stick moves a frame from
row to row; left and right change a choice or a slider, A presses. On
the page Input the frame goes from control to control: A changes it, X
clears it and Y sets it back to its default, on the device shown; while
Start mapping, A held a second skips a control (let go sooner, it is the
button the control takes). [menu]
gives them other inputs, as [controls.<game>] does: up, down, left,
right, press, back, clear, default, page_before, page_after, game_before,
game_after.

Odin writes every key it knows in odin.ini, at the start, those at their
value by default as comments ("# volume = 100"): taking the # away and
changing the value changes it. The menu writes a change in place, and
the comment again for a value set back to its default.

The cabinet has 1p_service and 2p_service, coin1 and coin2, test (of the
I/O board), board_test and board_service; a game without controls in the
list has 1p_start, 1p_up, 1p_down, 1p_left, 1p_right, 1p_push1 to 1p_push6,
and the same for 2p. The keys and pad inputs it starts with are in
src/odin/bindings.cpp; odin-games.json gives a few buttons their own keys
("keys") and pad inputs ("pad").

What the board keeps without power (the settings of the BIOS, the records
and settings of the game, the clock) is read at start and written on exit,
in a folder for each game: ~/.local/share/odin/saves/<game> (under
XDG_DATA_HOME when it is set), %APPDATA%\odin\saves\<game> on Windows,
~/Library/Application Support/odin/saves/<game> on macOS, or the folder
saves in [paths] of odin.ini (page Paths). ODIN_SAVES=<folder> takes
another folder before it, ODIN_SAVES=none keeps nothing. The files have the names
MAME gives them: mie_eeprom (the SYSTEM MENU of the BIOS: country, coins,
cabinet type, sound of the attract), nvram (the game), main_eeprom and
rombd_eeprom (the serial number of the main board, the security of the ROM
board). A game without mie_eeprom starts as a new U.S.A. cabinet left the
factory: the settings of its manual. Odin calibrates no control in
advance: the board of Air Trix steers once it is calibrated in the test
menu of the game (CALIBRATION), with the controller you play with, and
the other games have their own pages for it. The settings of Odin are in
odin.ini in the same odin folder; ODIN_CONFIG=<file> reads another one.

What Odin says (in the terminal it was started from; on Windows, opened by
a double click, it has none) is written to odin.log in the same odin folder
too, from scratch at each launch; the log of the launch before is kept as
odin-previous.log.
ODIN_LOG=<file> writes another file, ODIN_LOG=none none. The file stops at
64 MB (the detailed log of -v says a lot); after a crash it holds the last
words of Odin.

The comments at the top of the source files tell the other variables of the
environment; they are for debugging.
